<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh de Lacy, Earl of Ulster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1176–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh de Lacy, a younger son of the Lord of Meath, was made Earl of Ulster by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> in 1205 after he had driven out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-courcy">John de Courcy</a>. He lost his lands when John campaigned in Ireland in 1210, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, returning to recover the earldom by 1227. He died in 1242 without male heirs, and the earldom later passed to Walter de Burgh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-earl-of-ulster.jpg" alt="Hugh de Lacy, Earl of Ulster" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cookieman1.1.1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lacy_Coat_of_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
